; usbmsc.pi4 - USB Mass Storage, Bulk-Only Transport, over xhci.pi4.
;
; WHAT THIS IS FOR. The bench boots from a USB stick and always will.
; fat.pi4 has been able to parse FAT32 since the day it was written, and
; has been reading a fabricated image in memory because nothing could
; hand it real blocks. This file is the thing that hands it real blocks.
;
; The seam it plugs into is fat.pi4's, unchanged:
;
;     FatSetBlockReader(@MscReadBlock)
;
; MscReadBlock has exactly SdReadBlock's shape - (lba, *buf) -> 1 or 0 -
; because fat.pi4's author wrote that contract down and said in the
; header that a USB block source would be "one more procedure of the
; same shape and NOT ONE LINE OF THIS FILE CHANGES". It was, and it is.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHAT BULK-ONLY TRANSPORT ACTUALLY IS
; ======================================================================
;  USB Mass Storage Class Bulk-Only Transport, rev 1.0, and it is a
;  three-step conversation on two bulk endpoints:
;
;    1. the host writes a 31-byte Command Block Wrapper to bulk OUT
;    2. data moves, in whichever direction the CBW declared, if any
;    3. the device writes a 13-byte Command Status Wrapper to bulk IN
;
;  Every step is mandatory. Skipping the CSW read because the data
;  arrived leaves the device waiting to send it, and the NEXT command's
;  data phase then returns the previous command's status - which reads
;  as corruption rather than as a protocol error.
;
;  THE CBW, 31 bytes, all little-endian:
;
;     0   dCBWSignature        $43425355, the ASCII "USBC"
;     4   dCBWTag              echoed back in the CSW; used to detect
;                              a reply to a different command
;     8   dCBWDataTransferLength
;    12   bmCBWFlags           $80 = device to host, 0 = host to device
;    13   bCBWLUN              low four bits
;    14   bCBWCBLength         low five bits, 1..16
;    15   CBWCB[16]            the SCSI command itself
;
;  THE CSW, 13 bytes:
;
;     0   dCSWSignature        $53425355, the ASCII "USBS"
;     4   dCSWTag
;     8   dCSWDataResidue      bytes NOT transferred
;    12   bCSWStatus           0 pass, 1 fail, 2 phase error
;
;  A phase error is not a failed command. It means the two ends have
;  lost track of whose turn it is, and the only correct response is a
;  reset - which is why it gets its own error code here instead of
;  being folded into "command failed".
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE SCSI COMMANDS, AND WHY THESE SIX
; ======================================================================
;  The heading here said FOUR and listed four, while the file has always
;  defined six (#SCSI_* at :171-176). INQUIRY and WRITE (10) were both
;  missing from the prose. Corrected 2026-08-27 - a header that
;  undercounts what a file does is the same class of defect as one that
;  overstates what has been proven, and it is easier to fix than to
;  notice.
;  TEST UNIT READY ($00) - "are you there". Costs nothing and is the
;  only way to find out that a device needs its start-up time. A stick
;  that has just been powered will fail this several times before
;  passing, which is normal and NOT an error; MscInit retries.
;
;  REQUEST SENSE ($03) - why the last command failed. Mandatory after a
;  failure: many devices refuse everything else until the sense data is
;  collected. Skipping it turns one recoverable failure into a device
;  that never works again this session.
;
;  INQUIRY ($12) - vendor, product and revision strings. Not needed to
;  move a block, and worth its few lines anyway: it is what turns "a
;  medium answered" into "Samsung  Flash Drive FIT" on the console, and
;  a name is the difference between knowing which device replied and
;  assuming.
;
;  READ CAPACITY (10) ($25) - the last LBA and the block size, both
;  BIG-endian, which is the opposite of everything else here. SCSI is
;  big-endian and USB is little-endian and they meet in this file.
;
;  READ (10) ($28) - the original job.
;
;  WRITE (10) ($2A) - added later. MscWriteBlock() is the counterpart of
;  MscReadBlock() and has the same shape, so fat.pi4 takes it through
;  FatSetBlockWriter(@MscWriteBlock) exactly as it takes the reader.
;
;  THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY "Writing is deliberately absent. A
;  bootloader reads." That was the design and it stopped being true, and
;  it is worth a line about WHY, because the reason is not scope creep.
;  The bench boots from a USB stick and there is no second medium: the
;  radio needs three firmware files on that stick and not one of them is
;  there to be replaced, so without a writer the stick has to come out
;  and go into another machine - the exact dependency the monitor's
;  `save` exists to remove. A bootloader that cannot write is a
;  bootloader that needs hands.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHAT HAS RUN ON SILICON - and this file had no status section at all
; ======================================================================
;  It needed one. This is one of the most thoroughly exercised files in
;  the directory and a reader could not tell that from the header, which
;  is the same defect as a stale "nothing has run" banner with the sign
;  flipped: a file that says nothing about its own evidence gets weighed
;  by guesswork.
;
;  READING, 2026-08-26. Through a real VL805, a real xHCI controller and
;  a real Samsung Flash Drive FIT, at the monitor prompt:
;
;      mounted USB: Samsung  Flash Drive FIT   119 GB
;      medium    USB, 250626566 blocks of 512 bytes
;      volume    FAT32 on partition 1, cluster 4096 bytes
;
;  CONFIG.TXT came back at 813 bytes and matched the boot partition's
;  actual file; START4.ELF came back at 2,298,048 bytes.
;
;  WRITING, 2026-08-26. Three firmware files were created on that stick
;  through MscWriteBlock and each was read back off the volume into a
;  DIFFERENT address and CRC-checked on the board. The 609,309-byte
;  radio image later CRC'd to $B8B12CED - the value the host computed
;  before it was ever sent.
;
;  AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING AT EVERY BOOT: the firmware loads KERNEL8.IMG
;  off this stick, and everything the monitor loads afterwards comes
;  through here.
;
;  ---- THE BUG THAT COST THE MOST, because the shape recurs ----------
;
;  FALSE SHARING ON A CACHE LINE, and it wore three disguises first.
;  Every Bulk-Only command returned a status wrapper of thirteen ZERO
;  bytes while the controller correctly reported a thirteen-byte short
;  packet - so the device was answering and the answer was being
;  destroyed. It was blamed on SuperSpeed, then on short packets, then
;  on the retry loop supplying time. All three were wrong.
;
;  What settled it was asking for 64 bytes instead of 13: a real device
;  answers a short packet of exactly 13, and a fabricated completion
;  returns whatever was asked for. It returned 13.
;
;  msc_csw sat forty bytes into its cache line with the tail of msc_cbw
;  in the same line. msc_cbw is written by the CPU immediately before
;  each command and msc_csw by the DEVICE immediately after. ONE LINE,
;  TWO WRITERS - and no amount of cleaning or invalidating helps,
;  because both operate on the whole line. That is why the buffers below
;  are separate arrays rather than fields in a structure, and why the
;  comment under BUFFERS LIVE HERE says "cache-line aligned" rather than
;  merely "global".
;
;  ---- WHAT IS NOT PROVEN --------------------------------------------
;
;    * ONE DEVICE. Everything above is one Samsung stick on one port.
;      Start-up timing, LUN handling, sense-data recovery and phase
;      errors are all exercised only as far as that device provokes
;      them, and a phase error has never actually occurred.
;    * DURABILITY. A write that returns 1 has been ACCEPTED, not
;      necessarily committed to flash - there is no flush in the
;      Bulk-Only contract and none is issued here. fat.pi4's ordering
;      rules are only as good as this device's honesty about completion.
;    * NOTHING HAS BEEN INTERRUPTED MID-WRITE and inspected afterwards.
; ======================================================================
;  BUFFERS LIVE HERE, NOT ON THE STACK
; ======================================================================
;  Every buffer the controller touches is a global, because a bulk
;  transfer is DMA and the controller must be able to reach the memory
;  after this procedure's frame is gone. They are also cache-line
;  aligned by being separate arrays rather than fields in a structure.
;
;  Needs, in the main file: uart.pi4, timer.pi4, pcie.pi4, xhci.pi4.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Constants
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#MSC_CBW_BYTES = 31
#MSC_CSW_BYTES = 13
#MSC_CBW_SIG   = $43425355      ; "USBC"
#MSC_CSW_SIG   = $53425355      ; "USBS"
#MSC_DIR_IN    = $80            ; bmCBWFlags

#MSC_BLOCK_BYTES = 512          ; what fat.pi4's contract requires

#SCSI_TEST_UNIT_READY = $00
#SCSI_REQUEST_SENSE   = $03
#SCSI_INQUIRY         = $12
#SCSI_READ_CAPACITY10 = $25
#SCSI_READ10          = $28
#SCSI_WRITE10         = $2A

; How many times TEST UNIT READY may fail before the device is declared
; absent. A stick coming out of reset commonly needs a handful.
#MSC_READY_TRIES = 40

#MSC_ERR_NONE       = 0
#MSC_ERR_NOT_READY  = -1   ; the bulk endpoints were never configured
#MSC_ERR_CBW        = -2   ; the CBW did not go out whole
#MSC_ERR_DATA       = -3   ; the data phase moved the wrong amount
#MSC_ERR_CSW        = -4   ; no CSW, or a short one
#MSC_ERR_CSW_SIG    = -5   ; the CSW signature was not "USBS"
#MSC_ERR_CSW_TAG    = -6   ; the CSW answered a different command
#MSC_ERR_FAILED     = -7   ; bCSWStatus 1 - the command failed
#MSC_ERR_PHASE      = -8   ; bCSWStatus 2 - the ends disagree
#MSC_ERR_NO_MEDIUM  = -9   ; TEST UNIT READY never passed
#MSC_ERR_CAPACITY   = -10  ; READ CAPACITY returned nonsense
#MSC_ERR_BLOCKSIZE  = -11  ; the medium is not 512-byte blocks
#MSC_ERR_ARG        = -12  ; a caller mistake
#MSC_ERR_RANGE      = -13  ; the LBA is past the end of the medium

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  State. All DMA buffers are globals - see the header.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ======================================================================
;  EVERY DMA BUFFER GETS ITS OWN CACHE LINE. THIS IS NOT TIDINESS.
; ======================================================================
;  These used to be three plain global arrays, and the status wrapper
;  came back as thirteen zero bytes every single time while the
;  controller correctly reported a thirteen-byte short packet. The
;  device was answering; the answer was being destroyed.
;
;  The buffers landed at whatever address the linker chose. msc_csw was
;  at $480C68 - forty bytes into its 64-byte cache line - and the tail
;  of msc_cbw was in that same line. msc_cbw is written by the CPU
;  immediately before every command; msc_csw is written by the DEVICE
;  immediately after. One cache line, two writers, and the CPU's copy
;  wins whenever it is written back.
;
;  That is false sharing, and on a non-coherent DMA path it is not a
;  performance problem, it is data loss. No amount of cleaning and
;  invalidating fixes it, because the granularity of both operations is
;  the LINE: there is no way to write back the CBW half without also
;  writing back the CPU's stale idea of the CSW half.
;
;  The tell was that control transfers worked perfectly through the same
;  driver. Those DMA into the xhci arena, which is bump-allocated with
;  64-byte alignment - so its buffers never share a line with anything.
;  The rule was already there and these three globals were simply
;  outside it.
;
;  SO: one backing array, and every buffer carved out of it on a 64-byte
;  boundary with a whole number of lines each. The extra #MSC_ALIGN
;  bytes are the slack the run-time alignment needs, because the
;  compiler's BSS is only guaranteed 16.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#MSC_ALIGN     = 64
#MSC_OFF_CBW   = 0            ; 64 bytes - one line
#MSC_OFF_CSW   = 64           ; 64 bytes - one line
#MSC_OFF_DATA  = 128          ; 512 bytes - eight lines
#MSC_OFF_BLOCK = 640          ; 512 bytes - see MscReadBlock
#MSC_POOL_USED = 1152
#MSC_POOL_BYTES = 1216        ; #MSC_POOL_USED + #MSC_ALIGN of slack

Global Dim msc_pool.a[#MSC_POOL_BYTES]
Global msc_base.i = 0           ; the aligned base, 0 until MscBuffers()

; Carve the pool. Idempotent, and called from everything that needs a
; buffer so no caller can forget.
Procedure MscBuffers()
  If msc_base <> 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  msc_base = (@msc_pool[0] + (#MSC_ALIGN - 1)) / #MSC_ALIGN
  msc_base = msc_base * #MSC_ALIGN
EndProcedure

Procedure.i msc_Cbw()
  MscBuffers()
  ProcedureReturn msc_base + #MSC_OFF_CBW
EndProcedure

Procedure.i msc_Csw()
  MscBuffers()
  ProcedureReturn msc_base + #MSC_OFF_CSW
EndProcedure

Procedure.i msc_Data()
  MscBuffers()
  ProcedureReturn msc_base + #MSC_OFF_DATA
EndProcedure

Procedure.i msc_Block()
  MscBuffers()
  ProcedureReturn msc_base + #MSC_OFF_BLOCK
EndProcedure
Global msc_tag.i     = 0        ; increments per command
Global msc_err.i     = 0
Global msc_ready.i   = 0        ; 1 once capacity is known
Global msc_blocks.i  = 0        ; how many blocks the medium has
Global msc_blockSz.i = 0
Global msc_lastStatus.i = 0     ; the last bCSWStatus seen
Global msc_senseKey.i   = 0     ; from the last REQUEST SENSE
Global msc_senseAsc.i   = 0
Global msc_lastIn.i     = 0   ; bytes the last CSW read reported
Global msc_firstErr.i   = 0   ; why the FIRST TEST UNIT READY failed.
                              ; The fortieth failure is never the
                              ; interesting one; the first is.
Global Dim msc_vendor.a[16]     ; INQUIRY strings, NUL-terminated
Global Dim msc_product.a[24]

Procedure.i msc_Fail(code.i)
  msc_err = code
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; DIAGNOSTIC ACCESSORS. The wrappers are the protocol, and when the two
; ends fall out of step the only way to see why is to look at the bytes
; that actually arrived rather than at a decoded field.
Procedure.i MscCswAddr() : ProcedureReturn msc_Csw() : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscCbwAddr() : ProcedureReturn msc_Cbw() : EndProcedure

Procedure.i MscError()
  ProcedureReturn msc_err
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Little-endian and big-endian helpers.
;
;  Spelled out per byte rather than done with a 32-bit poke, because the
;  two orders live side by side in this file - USB is little-endian and
;  SCSI is big-endian - and a reader has to be able to see which is
;  which at the point of use.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure msc_PutLe32(*p, off.i, v.i)
  PokeA(*p + off + 0, v & $FF)
  PokeA(*p + off + 1, (v >> 8) & $FF)
  PokeA(*p + off + 2, (v >> 16) & $FF)
  PokeA(*p + off + 3, (v >> 24) & $FF)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i msc_GetLe32(*p, off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekA(*p + off + 0) | (PeekA(*p + off + 1) << 8) | (PeekA(*p + off + 2) << 16) | (PeekA(*p + off + 3) << 24)
EndProcedure

Procedure msc_PutBe32(*p, off.i, v.i)
  PokeA(*p + off + 0, (v >> 24) & $FF)
  PokeA(*p + off + 1, (v >> 16) & $FF)
  PokeA(*p + off + 2, (v >> 8) & $FF)
  PokeA(*p + off + 3, v & $FF)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i msc_GetBe32(*p, off.i)
  ProcedureReturn (PeekA(*p + off + 0) << 24) | (PeekA(*p + off + 1) << 16) | (PeekA(*p + off + 2) << 8) | PeekA(*p + off + 3)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  msc_Command - one whole CBW / data / CSW exchange.
;
;  *cdb is the SCSI command, cdbLen its length. dir is #MSC_DIR_IN or 0.
;  *buf/len describe the data phase and may be 0/0.
;
;  Returns 1 on a command that both transferred and reported success.
;  Anything else sets msc_err and returns 0 - including a command that
;  moved its data perfectly and then reported failure, because that is
;  what bCSWStatus is for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i msc_Command(*cdb, cdbLen.i, dir.i, *buf, len.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  Define sig.i
  Define tag.i

  If XhciBulkReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  If cdbLen < 1 Or cdbLen > 16
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_ARG)
  EndIf

  msc_tag = msc_tag + 1

  ; ---- the CBW ------------------------------------------------------
  i = 0
  While i < #MSC_CBW_BYTES
    PokeA(msc_Cbw() + i, 0)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  msc_PutLe32(msc_Cbw(), 0, #MSC_CBW_SIG)
  msc_PutLe32(msc_Cbw(), 4, msc_tag)
  msc_PutLe32(msc_Cbw(), 8, len)
  PokeA(msc_Cbw() + 12, dir & $80)
  PokeA(msc_Cbw() + 13, 0)              ; LUN 0
  PokeA(msc_Cbw() + 14, cdbLen & $1F)
  i = 0
  While i < cdbLen
    PokeA(msc_Cbw() + 15 + i, PeekA(*cdb + i))
    i = i + 1
  Wend

  n = XhciBulkOut(msc_Cbw(), #MSC_CBW_BYTES)
  If n <> #MSC_CBW_BYTES
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CBW)
  EndIf

  ; ---- the data phase, if any ---------------------------------------
  If len > 0 And *buf <> 0
    If (dir & #MSC_DIR_IN) <> 0
      n = XhciBulkIn(*buf, len)
    Else
      n = XhciBulkOut(*buf, len)
    EndIf
    ; A SHORT data phase is not automatically an error - the residue in
    ; the CSW is the authority - but a NEGATIVE one is, and so is a
    ; stall. Let the CSW below decide about a short read; refuse only
    ; on a transfer that did not happen.
    If n < 0
      ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_DATA)
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; ---- the CSW ------------------------------------------------------
  ; ALWAYS read it, even after a failed data phase. The device has one
  ; queued and will hand it to the next command's data phase otherwise.
  ; ASK FOR MORE THAN A STATUS WRAPPER IS, ON PURPOSE.
  ;
  ; A CSW is 13 bytes. Requesting exactly 13 makes "the device sent 13"
  ; and "the controller invented 13 because that is what we asked for"
  ; indistinguishable - and the buffer was arriving as zeros with the
  ; transfer reporting success, so telling those apart is the whole
  ; question. Request 64 and a real device answers with a SHORT PACKET
  ; of exactly 13; anything reporting 64 moved is not the device
  ; talking.
  n = XhciBulkIn(msc_Csw(), 64)
  msc_lastIn = n
  If n < #MSC_CSW_BYTES
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CSW)
  EndIf
  sig = msc_GetLe32(msc_Csw(), 0)
  If sig <> #MSC_CSW_SIG
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CSW_SIG)
  EndIf
  tag = msc_GetLe32(msc_Csw(), 4)
  If tag <> msc_tag
    ; The reply belongs to an earlier command. Continuing would attach
    ; this status to the wrong request.
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CSW_TAG)
  EndIf

  msc_lastStatus = PeekA(msc_Csw() + 12)
  If msc_lastStatus = 1
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_FAILED)
  EndIf
  If msc_lastStatus = 2
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_PHASE)
  EndIf

  msc_err = #MSC_ERR_NONE
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscRequestSense - collect the sense data after a failure.
;
;  Called by MscInit's retry loop. Many devices will refuse every
;  command until this is done, so a driver that skips it after the first
;  failure never recovers.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscRequestSense()
  Dim cdb.a[16]
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < 16
    cdb[i] = 0
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  cdb[0] = #SCSI_REQUEST_SENSE
  cdb[4] = 18
  If msc_Command(@cdb[0], 6, #MSC_DIR_IN, msc_Data(), 18) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; Sense key is the low nibble of byte 2; the additional sense code is
  ; byte 12. Together they say things like "not ready, becoming ready",
  ; which is the normal answer from a stick that is still spinning up.
  msc_senseKey = PeekA(msc_Data() + 2) & $0F
  msc_senseAsc = PeekA(msc_Data() + 12)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MscLastIn() : ProcedureReturn msc_lastIn : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscFirstError() : ProcedureReturn msc_firstErr : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscSenseKey()  : ProcedureReturn msc_senseKey : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscSenseAsc()  : ProcedureReturn msc_senseAsc : EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscTestUnitReady - one poll. 1 if the unit answered ready.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscTestUnitReady()
  Dim cdb.a[16]
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < 16
    cdb[i] = 0
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  cdb[0] = #SCSI_TEST_UNIT_READY
  ProcedureReturn msc_Command(@cdb[0], 6, 0, 0, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscInquiry - who is this. Fills the vendor and product strings.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscInquiry()
  Dim cdb.a[16]
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < 16
    cdb[i] = 0
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  cdb[0] = #SCSI_INQUIRY
  cdb[4] = 36
  If msc_Command(@cdb[0], 6, #MSC_DIR_IN, msc_Data(), 36) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; Bytes 8..15 are the vendor and 16..31 the product, space-padded
  ; ASCII with no terminator. Copied out and terminated here so callers
  ; can print them without knowing that.
  i = 0
  While i < 8
    msc_vendor[i] = PeekA(msc_Data() + 8 + i)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  msc_vendor[8] = 0
  i = 0
  While i < 16
    msc_product[i] = PeekA(msc_Data() + 16 + i)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  msc_product[16] = 0
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MscVendor()  : ProcedureReturn @msc_vendor[0]  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscProduct() : ProcedureReturn @msc_product[0] : EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscReadCapacity - the last LBA and the block size, BOTH BIG-ENDIAN.
;
;  READ CAPACITY(10) returns the address of the LAST block, not the
;  count. Off by one here means the last block of the medium is
;  unreadable, which on a FAT volume is usually in the middle of the
;  data area and presents as one corrupt file.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscReadCapacity()
  Dim cdb.a[16]
  Define i.i
  Define lastLba.i
  i = 0
  While i < 16
    cdb[i] = 0
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  cdb[0] = #SCSI_READ_CAPACITY10
  If msc_Command(@cdb[0], 10, #MSC_DIR_IN, msc_Data(), 8) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  lastLba     = msc_GetBe32(msc_Data(), 0) & $FFFFFFFF
  msc_blockSz = msc_GetBe32(msc_Data(), 4) & $FFFFFFFF
  If msc_blockSz <= 0 Or lastLba <= 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CAPACITY)
  EndIf
  ; fat.pi4's contract is 512-byte blocks. A 4096-byte medium is a real
  ; thing and would need the filesystem layer to know; refusing is the
  ; honest answer rather than reading a quarter of every cluster.
  If msc_blockSz <> #MSC_BLOCK_BYTES
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_BLOCKSIZE)
  EndIf
  msc_blocks = lastLba + 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MscBlockCount() : ProcedureReturn msc_blocks  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscBlockSize()  : ProcedureReturn msc_blockSz : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscReady()      : ProcedureReturn msc_ready   : EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscInit - bring the logical unit up. The bulk endpoints must already
;  be configured by XhciConfigureBulk.
;
;  Returns 1, or 0 with MscError() set.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscInit()
  Define tries.i

  msc_ready = 0
  msc_blocks = 0
  msc_blockSz = 0
  If XhciBulkReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf

  ; TEST UNIT READY, repeatedly. The first few failures are expected on
  ; a device that has just been reset and are NOT reported as errors -
  ; only running out of patience is. Sense data is collected after each
  ; failure because some devices will not proceed until it is.
  ; LET THE DEVICE SETTLE AFTER SET_CONFIGURATION.
  ;
  ; The very first Bulk-Only exchange after Configure Endpoint came back
  ; with a status wrapper that was not USBS, every time, on two different
  ; code paths - and every exchange after it worked. A probe that
  ; happened to issue one throwaway command first "worked", which is a
  ; horrible thing to depend on and was the clue: the retry loop was
  ; quietly supplying time, not recovering from anything.
  ;
  ; USB 2.0 section 9.2.6.4 allows a device up to 5 ms of recovery after
  ; a SET_CONFIGURATION before it must respond. Nothing above waits, so
  ; the wait is here, once, before the first command. Generous rather
  ; than minimal: this happens exactly once per device and the cost of
  ; being wrong is a device that never works.
  delay(50)

  tries = 0
  msc_firstErr = 0
  While tries < #MSC_READY_TRIES
    If MscTestUnitReady() = 1
      Break
    EndIf
    ; KEEP THE FIRST REASON. Reporting only "never became ready" after
    ; forty attempts hides whether the very first one failed in the
    ; command wrapper, the data phase or the status phase - and those
    ; are three unrelated faults that a retry loop turns into one
    ; useless sentence.
    If tries = 0
      msc_firstErr = msc_err
    EndIf
    MscRequestSense()
    delay(50)
    tries = tries + 1
  Wend
  If tries >= #MSC_READY_TRIES
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_NO_MEDIUM)
  EndIf

  If MscInquiry() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If MscReadCapacity() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  msc_ready = 1
  msc_err = #MSC_ERR_NONE
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; A 192-byte scratch for one configuration descriptor. Small, and its
; own so MscSetupCurrent does not borrow the command data buffer that a
; concurrent read might be using.
Global Dim msc_cfg.a[192]
Global msc_slot.i = 0           ; the xHCI slot the stick was set up on

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscSetupCurrent - is the CURRENTLY ADDRESSED xHCI device a USB mass-
;  storage stick, and if so, make it usable. Returns 1 if this slot is
;  now a working block device, 0 if it is not mass storage (leave it for
;  the caller to try as HID) or could not be brought up.
;
;  THIS IS WHAT LETS THE MOUSE, THE KEYBOARD AND THE STICK SHARE ONE
;  CONTROLLER. The enumeration walk addresses every device once; for
;  each it offers the slot here, and a stick is configured on the spot
;  without a second controller bring-up. Bulk transfers already target
;  xh_mscSlot (set by XhciConfigureBulk) and HID control transfers target
;  the per-poll xh_slot, so the two never collide once both are up.
;
;  NO PRINTING - it runs inside the walk and a device that is simply not
;  a stick is not news.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscSetupCurrent()
  Define got.i
  Define total.i
  Define cfgVal.i
  Define off.i
  Define len.i
  Define typ.i
  Define cls.i
  Define sub.i
  Define prot.i
  Define epOut.i
  Define epIn.i
  Define mpsOut.i
  Define mpsIn.i

  ; A stick is already set up. If it is this same slot, done; a second
  ; stick on another slot is not something one bulk pair can serve.
  If XhciBulkReady() <> 0
    If XhciBulkReady() = XhciSlot()
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  got = XhciGetDescriptor(2, 0, 0, 64)
  If got < 9
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If XhciCopyIn(@msc_cfg[0], got) <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  total = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + 2) | (PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + 3) << 8)
  If total > got
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  cfgVal = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + 5)

  cls = 0
  sub = 0
  prot = 0
  epOut = 0
  epIn = 0
  mpsOut = 0
  mpsIn = 0
  off = 0
  While off < total
    len = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off)
    typ = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 1)
    If len = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    If typ = 4
      cls  = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 5)
      sub  = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 6)
      prot = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 7)
    ElseIf typ = 5 And cls = 8
      If (PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 3) & 3) = 2
        If (PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 2) & $80) <> 0
          epIn  = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 2)
          mpsIn = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 4) | (PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 5) << 8)
        Else
          epOut  = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 2)
          mpsOut = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 4) | (PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 5) << 8)
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
    off = off + len
  Wend

  ; SCSI transparent (6) over Bulk-Only ($50). Anything else on class 8
  ; is not something MscInit's command set can speak to.
  If cls <> 8 Or sub <> 6 Or prot <> $50
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If epOut = 0 Or epIn = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If XhciSetConfiguration(cfgVal) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If XhciConfigureBulk(epOut, mpsOut, epIn, mpsIn) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If MscInit() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  msc_slot = XhciSlot()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscReadBlock - fat.pi4's block reader, exactly.
;
;      Procedure.i Reader(lba.i, *buf)   -> 1 or 0
;
;  Pass @MscReadBlock to FatSetBlockReader and the filesystem is reading
;  the USB stick. Nothing in fat.pi4 changes, which is what its seam was
;  built for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscReadBlock(lba.i, *buf)
  Dim cdb.a[16]
  Define i.i

  If *buf = 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_ARG)
  EndIf
  If msc_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  If lba < 0 Or lba >= msc_blocks
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_RANGE)
  EndIf

  i = 0
  While i < 16
    cdb[i] = 0
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  cdb[0] = #SCSI_READ10
  ; The LBA is big-endian at bytes 2..5 and the block COUNT is
  ; big-endian at bytes 7..8. One block.
  msc_PutBe32(@cdb[0], 2, lba)
  cdb[7] = 0
  cdb[8] = 1

  ; BOUNCE THROUGH AN ALIGNED BUFFER. *buf belongs to the caller - for
  ; fat.pi4 it is a sector cache inside that file - and nothing here can
  ; promise it is cache-line aligned, or that it does not share a line
  ; with something the CPU writes. That is exactly the false sharing
  ; that made the status wrapper arrive as thirteen zero bytes; see the
  ; buffer pool at the top. A 512-byte copy is nothing beside a USB
  ; transfer, and it means a caller cannot get this wrong.
  If msc_Command(@cdb[0], 10, #MSC_DIR_IN, msc_Block(), #MSC_BLOCK_BYTES) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  i = 0
  While i < #MSC_BLOCK_BYTES
    PokeA(*buf + i, PeekA(msc_Block() + i))
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscWriteBlock - the counterpart of MscReadBlock. Same shape.
;
;  WRITING WAS DELIBERATELY ABSENT UNTIL NOW, and the header above still
;  says "a bootloader reads". That was true while Anvil was loaded by
;  U-Boot. It stopped being true the moment Anvil became the boot image:
;  updating Anvil then means writing to the medium it boots from, and
;  the alternative is a second machine and a pulled stick every time.
;
;  IT IS STILL NOT A GENERAL WRITE PATH. There is no cluster allocation
;  here and none above it; see FatSaveInPlace. This writes one block
;  that something else has already decided belongs to the file.
;
;  WRITE(10) is READ(10) with a different opcode and the data going the
;  other way. The LBA is big-endian at bytes 2..5 and the block count
;  big-endian at 7..8, exactly as for the read.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscWriteBlock(lba.i, *buf)
  Dim cdb.a[16]
  Define i.i

  If *buf = 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_ARG)
  EndIf
  If msc_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  If lba < 0 Or lba >= msc_blocks
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_RANGE)
  EndIf

  ; Through the aligned bounce buffer, for the same reason the read is:
  ; the caller's memory carries no cache-line promise, and a DMA buffer
  ; sharing a line with something the CPU writes loses data. On the way
  ; out the danger is the mirror image of the read's - our own stale
  ; line written back over what we meant to send.
  i = 0
  While i < #MSC_BLOCK_BYTES
    PokeA(msc_Block() + i, PeekA(*buf + i))
    i = i + 1
  Wend

  i = 0
  While i < 16
    cdb[i] = 0
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  cdb[0] = #SCSI_WRITE10
  msc_PutBe32(@cdb[0], 2, lba)
  cdb[7] = 0
  cdb[8] = 1

  ProcedureReturn msc_Command(@cdb[0], 10, 0, msc_Block(), #MSC_BLOCK_BYTES)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscErrorText - one sentence per code, naming the STAGE.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscErrorText()
  Select msc_err
    Case #MSC_ERR_NONE
      ProcedureReturn "msc: ok"
    Case #MSC_ERR_NOT_READY
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the bulk endpoints are not configured - XhciConfigureBulk first"
    Case #MSC_ERR_CBW
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the 31-byte command wrapper did not go out whole"
    Case #MSC_ERR_DATA
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the data phase failed"
    Case #MSC_ERR_CSW
      ProcedureReturn "msc: no status wrapper came back, or it was short"
    Case #MSC_ERR_CSW_SIG
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the status wrapper's signature was not USBS - the two ends are out of step"
    Case #MSC_ERR_CSW_TAG
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the status wrapper answered a different command"
    Case #MSC_ERR_FAILED
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the device reported the command failed"
    Case #MSC_ERR_PHASE
      ProcedureReturn "msc: phase error - the device and host disagree about whose turn it is, and only a reset fixes that"
    Case #MSC_ERR_NO_MEDIUM
      ; Name the FIRST failure, not the last. See MscFirstError.
      Select msc_firstErr
        Case #MSC_ERR_CSW_SIG
          ProcedureReturn "msc: the unit never became ready, and the first status wrapper was not USBS - the bulk endpoints are not carrying data"
        Case #MSC_ERR_CSW
          ProcedureReturn "msc: the unit never became ready, and no status wrapper came back at all"
        Case #MSC_ERR_CBW
          ProcedureReturn "msc: the unit never became ready, and the command wrapper would not go out"
        Case #MSC_ERR_CSW_TAG
          ProcedureReturn "msc: the unit never became ready, and the status wrappers are answering earlier commands - the two ends are one reply apart"
        Case #MSC_ERR_FAILED
          ProcedureReturn "msc: the unit reported not-ready every time - it is talking, but it has no medium"
      EndSelect
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the unit never became ready"
    Case #MSC_ERR_CAPACITY
      ProcedureReturn "msc: READ CAPACITY returned a size that cannot be right"
    Case #MSC_ERR_BLOCKSIZE
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the medium does not use 512-byte blocks, which the filesystem layer requires"
    Case #MSC_ERR_ARG
      ProcedureReturn "msc: bad argument"
    Case #MSC_ERR_RANGE
      ProcedureReturn "msc: that block is past the end of the medium"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "msc: unknown error"
EndProcedure
